      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate document carefully.\n\n**Document-level info:**\n- Number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02 revision 3\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 3846781\n- Page total: 6\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 30 July 2024\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (from \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\")\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V. (OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n- Address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- Phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1\n\n**Applicants:**\n- KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- Same as applicant (KROHNE Altometer). Also production locations include KROHNE Measurement Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd. — that's a production location. The schema has manufacturers[]. Should I include the Shanghai one? The certificate says \"Applicant and Manufacturer: KROHNE Altometer\". Production locations are listed separately. Hmm. The production locations could be considered manufacturers. Rule 12 says parse party layout variations. I'll include KROHNE Altometer as manufacturer, and maybe also add the Shanghai production location as a manufacturer with a note. Actually, let me be careful — \"Production location\" section says the water meter is produced at one of the following production locations. These are effectively manufacturing sites. I'll include both, since they're manufacturing locations. Actually the \"manufacturers\" is separate from \"production_locations\" in schema — schema doesn't have production_locations. I could include the Shanghai one as a manufacturer entry. That seems reasonable. Or keep it in a note. I think including it in manufacturers is the best fit given the schema.\n\nHmm, but rule 8 says don't fabricate. The certificate explicitly says \"Applicant and Manufacturer\" for KROHNE Altometer. The Shanghai location is a \"production