documentation_file | evaluation_report. These are \"associated reports\" — the OIML Type Evaluation Report is referenced. I'd call them type_evaluation_report.\n\nPages field: \"41 pages and 14 annexes\" — pages: 41. There's no annexes field in schema but I could include in... schema says id, date, pages, role. I'll stick with those; maybe include annexes as extra? Rule: conform to schema. I'll add pages only. Hmm, could add \"annexes\" but schema doesn't list it. Better to keep to schema. Actually the schema says \"test_reports[]: id, date, pages, role\" — I'll keep exactly those.\n\n**Documentation:**\n- \"Documentation folder no. T10201-11\" — that could go in test_reports with role documentation_file? It's a documentation folder, id T10201-11. Yes, include as test_reports entry with role \"documentation_file\". Date unknown — omit.\n\n**Revision history:**\n- Initial, 26 January 2021, \"-\" → changes: null or \"-\"\n- 1, 3 May 2022, \"Added alternative Modbus PCB components\"\n- 2, 12 June 2023, \"Added Alternative Micro Controller Unit (MCU)\"\n- 3, 30 July 2024, \"Added second production location\"\n\nRevision as string: \"Initial\", \"1\", \"2\", \"3\".\n\n**Characteristics (Table 1):**\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\" (string value)\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" → maybe string \"1 and 2\" or list. Type-level: value \"1 and 2\". Hmm, or [\"1\",\"2\"] — but the value shape allows number|min/max|string. I'll use \"1 and 2\" as string. Also recommendation.accuracy_classes: [\"1\",\"2\"].\n- environmental_class: \"M2 O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher; B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\" — this is configuration-dependent (software version). Could be config_level with axis \"software_version\". Let me use config_level for the software-dependent attributes:\n  - environmental_class: axis software_version: conditions \"5.0.1_ or higher\" → \"O (installed outdoors)\", \"4.3.1_ or lower\" → \"B (installed in a building)\". Also M2 overall? Actually \"Environmental class M2\" — wait, R49 environmental classes are O, M, B... Actually the table cell says \"M2<br>O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher<br>B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\". Hmm, that's odd formatting. In R49-1, environmental classes are: O (outdoors), M (mounted... ), B (building). Actually R49-1:2013 environmental classes: O = open air/outdoors, M = ... hmm. Actually the classes in R49-1:2013 Annex A... Let me recall: Environmental class O: installed outdoors; class B: installed in a building; class M: mounted on a vehicle or in a mobile installation? Something like that. The \"M2\" might be part of \"M2\" mechanical environment? Actually R49-1 references environmental classes from OIML D11: mechanical environment class M2, climatic... Hmm. Actually maybe the cell means \"M2\" as mechanical environment class and O/B as R49 environmental class. Hmm, the row is \"Environmental class\" and value \"M2 / O ... / B ...\". \n\nHmm wait, in R49-1:2013 there is Table A.1 with environmental classes: O (open air), B (building), M (mobile). And OIML D 11 defines mechanical M1/M2/M3 and climatic C1..C4 and electromagnetic E1..E5. The electromagnetic environment row says E2, which is a D11 class. So the environmental class row with M2... could actually be mechanical environment M2? But it's under \"Environmental class\". OCR might have merged. I'll just record as-is: environmental_class with value... The layout: \"M2\" then two software-version-dependent entries O and B. Hmm.\n\nI think best: type_level environmental_class = \"M2\"? That seems wrong semantically. Alternatively config_level:\n- environmental_class, axis \"software_version\": [{condition: \"5.0.1_ or higher\", value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}, {condition: \"4.3.1_ or lower\", value: \"B (installed in a building)\"}]\n\nAnd mechanical environment? Not mentioned elsewhere. I could just record the raw cell. Hmm, risk of over-interpretation. Let me record:\n- type_level: environmental_class: \"M2\" — no wait.\n\nLet me re-read: \"Environmental class: M2<br><b>O (installed outdoors)</b> for software version 5.0.1_ or higher<br><b>B (installed in a building)</b> for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\".\n\nHmm, in R49-1:2013, environmental classes per D11 in the informative annex... Actually I recall the NMi WATERFLUX 3070 certificate: \"Environmental class O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1 or higher; B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1 or lower\". And there's likely a separate \"Mechanical environment M2\"? Hmm, but the table here doesn't show a separate mechanical environment row. Actually maybe the \"M2\" belongs to mechanical environment class but was merged in OCR into the environmental class row. To be faithful, I can record:\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute \"environmental_class\", axis \"software_version\": values [{condition: \"5.0.1_ or higher\", value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}, {condition: \"4.3.1_ or lower\", value: \"B (installed in a building)\"}]\n\ntype_level:\n- mechanical_environment? No, don't fabricate. Hmm but \"M2\" is in the doc. I could record environmental_class... ugh. Alternatively record type_level \"environment_class_m2\"? \n\nI think safest: include config_level environmental_class with the two software-version conditions, and also a type_level entry environmental_class_additional or... no. Let me think about what an extractor should do. The cell content literally: \"M2\", \"O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", \"B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\". \n\nGiven R49-1:2013 terminology: \"Environmental class: O / B / M\" where O = outdoors etc. And mechanical environment classes M1, M2, M3 from D11. The NMi cert likely had rows: \"Environmental class: O ... / B ...\" and \"Mechanical environment: M2\". The OCR merged them. Actually looking at the actual WATERFLUX 3070 OIML certificate (R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02), I believe Table 1 rows: Measuring principle Electromagnetic; Accuracy class 1 and 2; Mechanical environment M2? Hmm.\n\nGiven ambiguity, I'll record:\n- type_level: mechanical_environment... no, can't confirm label.\n\nAlternative: record environmental_class as config_level with three values? conditions would be weird for M2.\n\nDecision: config_level attribute \"environmental_class\" axis \"software_version\" with the two entries; and type_level attribute \"environmental_class_m2\"? That's ugly. \n\nActually — hmm, maybe \"M2\" here is genuinely part of it: R49-1 environmental classes in the 2013 edition Table: \"Environmental class\" can be \"O\", \"M\", or \"B\" per clause ... no.\n\nWait, actually I remember now: OIML R49-1:2013 Annex A (Environmental tests) references environmental classes per OIML D 11: mechanical (M1/M2/M3), climatic (C1-C4), electromagnetic (E1-E5). And separately R49 defines environmental classes O, M, B for \"location classes\"? Let me recall R49-1:2013 clause 4.3: \"Environmental classes: Class O: closed/sheltered? ...\" Hmm. R49-1:2013 4.3: The instrument shall be classified as one of: class O (installed outdoors), class M (mounted in a vehicle), class B (installed in a building). Yes! R49-1 defines classes O, M, B for environmental conditions of use (4.3.2 \"Environmental classes\"?). So \"Environmental class: O ... / B ...\" makes sense with R49 classes. Then \"M2\" is likely mechanical environment class from D11 (M2). Hmm, but wait — the electromagnetic environment is E2, which is D11. So the cert lists D11 classes. Maybe there were rows \"Mechanical environment M2\", \"Electromagnetic environment E2\" and the climatic... The temperature range row covers climatic.\n\nOK final decision: I'll include:\n- config_level: environmental_class axis software_version: O (installed outdoors) for 5.0.1_ or higher; B (installed in a building) for 4.3.1_ or lower.\n- type_level: mechanical_environment: \"M2\"? I'm not 100% sure the M2 is mechanical environment. Risk: fabricating label. Alternatively type_level \"environmental_class_m2\"... also fabrication.\n\nCompromise: I'll put environmental_class as a config_level entry and also add a type_level entry \"mechanical_environment_class\" with value \"M2\"? Hmm.\n\nActually, you know, given the certificate structure, maybe the intended reading is: \"Environmental class: M2\" — no wait, M2 is not an R49 environmental class... Actually hold on. Let me reconsider: maybe in the original PDF the row is \"Environmental class: O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher / B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\" and \"M2\" is a stray from a row like \"Mechanical environment class: M2\". The actual WATERFLUX cert (I've seen similar NMi certs) has rows: \"Climatic environment: C2\"? Not sure.\n\nGiven uncertainty, the most faithful approach preserving the source: put the whole thing in one characteristic? The schema wants single values though. Values can be strings. I could do type_level: environmental_class: value \"M2; O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher; B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\". That's ugly but faithful.\n\nBetter: use config_level for the software-dependent part and type_level for \"M2\"? With label... The row label is \"Environmental class\". So type_level environmental_class... conflicts.\n\nI'll go with:\n- type_level: {label: \"environmental_class\", value: \"M2\"} — no.\n\nHmm, let me look at the actual certificate if I can recall. \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\" WATERFLUX 3070. I genuinely recall NMi certificates for this product: Table 1 includes \"Environmental class O / B\" split by software version — this matches revision history (rev 2 added alternative MCU etc.). I don't specifically recall M2. But given E2 appears separately (electromagnetic environment E2 is a D11 class), it's plausible the original had \"Mechanical environment: M2\" as its own row that got merged. Wait, the table shows \"Electromagnetic environment: E2\" as a separate row. If mechanical environment M2 existed it'd likely be its own row too. But OCR merged it into environmental class. Plausible.\n\nGiven the layered schema's flexibility, I'll do:\n- config_level: attribute \"environmental_class\", axis \"software_version\", values: [{condition: \"5.0.1_ or higher\", value: \"O (installed outdoors)\"}, {condition: \"4.3.1_ or lower\", value: \"B (installed in a building)\"}]\n- type_level: attribute \"mechanical_environment\", value \"M2\" — hmm, risk.\n\nAlternatively I can put \"M2\" into the environmental_class config as condition \"all\"? Meh.\n\nActually, wait. Reading the R49-1:2013 standard: Table 3 in clause 4? Let me think... R49-1:2013 clause 4.3 \"Environmental classes\": \"O: Open air (installed outdoors)\", \"M: ...\", \"B: Building\". Hmm no, I think it's: class O = outdoors, class B = indoors, class M = mobile. So \"Environmental class O/B\" fits.\n\nAnd OIML D11 classes M1/M2/M3 = mechanical environment (static/locally mobile/mobile), E1-E5 electromagnetic. The cert row \"Electromagnetic environment: E2\" is D11. So mechanical environment M2 likely its own row in original.\n\nDecision: include type_level \"mechanical_environment\": {\"value\": \"M2\"} — labeled as best interpretation. I think that's reasonable and low-risk. Actually the rule says \"Don't fabricate. If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" M2 IS present in the markdown, just with an ambiguous row label. Labeling it mechanical_environment is an interpretation. Given E2 is electromagnetic environment, M2 as mechanical environment is the standard pairing in OIML certs. I'll go with it.\n\nHmm, alternatively, to be maximally faithful without interpretation risk, I could include the full raw cell as environmental_class... I'll make a judgment: mechanical_environment: M2 as type_level; environmental_class as config_level.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\" (type_level, string)\n- temperature_range_ambient: config_level, axis software_version:\n  - condition \"5.0.1_ or higher\": value {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C\n  - condition \"4.3.1_ or lower\": value {min: -10, max: 55}, unit °C\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C)\" → water_temperature_class: value \"T50\", and maybe water_temperature_range: {min 0.1, max 50} °C. The \"+0,1 °C\" seems odd — probably \"0,1 °C / +50 °C\"? T50 class means water temp up to 50°C. Min water temp 0.1°C. So: water_temperature_class: \"T50\"; water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C. Decimal conversion: 0,1 → 0.1. Good.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure (MAP): 1,6 MPa (16 bar) → value 1.6, unit MPa; plus alternative 16 bar. I'll record map as value 1.6 unit \"MPa\", and maybe map_alternative 16 bar. I could do one entry with value \"1.6 MPa (16 bar)\"? Better structured: maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa. And a second characteristic maximum_admissible_pressure_alternative: 16 bar? Or use footnote? I'll add two entries: \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" {1.6, MPa} and \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\" hmm. Actually simpler: record MAP once with unit MPa value 1.6; the (16 bar) is redundant conversion. I might include it as a second value in the same? Schema single value. I'll include just 1.6 MPa, or both as separate characteristics. Let me include \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" = 1.6 MPa and also \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alternative_unit\" = 16 bar? That's noisy. I'll include both — actually rules say separate characteristics for split values (like voltage/frequency). I'll do map_mpa and... no. Keep it: maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}. And optionally a footnote? No. I'll also add second entry \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" can't duplicate... Fine, single entry. Hmm, but losing 16 bar. Add entry \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alt\": {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}? I'll skip to reduce noise. Actually, hmm, 16 bar is useful. I'll include it as part of a \"notes\"... no. Skip.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — I could record value as string \"1.6 MPa (16 bar)\" but that violates strict unit separation. Keep 1.6 MPa. Maybe mention in description? No. Fine.\n\n- orientation: \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\" → string value. Label: orientation. Value: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\".\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\" → string. Or split: flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0\"; straight_length_requirement: \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\". I'll do two entries: flow_profile_sensitivity_class \"U0 and D0\"; and maybe upstream_downstream_straight_lengths \"0 x DN upstream / 0 x DN downstream\". Keep as one: value \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\". Hmm, I'll split for cleanliness: flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0\"; zero_dn_straight_length_required... meh. Single string is fine and faithful.\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is designed to measure reverse flow\" → boolean? value true? Or string. I'll use string \"Sensor designed to measure reverse flow\" or value: true with... The value shape allows string. \"reverse_flow_supported\": true is nicer. I'll do reverse_flow: value true? Hmm, value true (boolean) — schema says number | {min,max} | string. Boolean not listed. Use string \"designed to measure reverse flow\" or \"true\". I'll use string.\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp 63 (0,63 bar)\" → pressure_loss_class: \"Δp63\"; pressure_loss: 0.63 bar. Split: pressure_loss_class: value \"63\" (class Δp63)? I'll do pressure_loss_class: \"Δp63\" and pressure_loss_max: {0.63, bar}. Good.\n- power_supply: config or alternatives: \n  - \"3,6 Volts D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\" OR\n  - Flexpower (software 5.0.1_ or higher): 3.6 V D-cell Lithium non-replaceable battery OR 9…30 VDC OR 100…230 VAC / 50-60Hz (+/-2%)\n  \n  This is complex. I'll record:\n  - type_level? No — model_level? It's software-version dependent. Options:\n    - power_supply_battery: {value: \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium (replaceable)\"} as base option\n    - config_level attribute \"power_supply\" axis \"software_version\"? The Flexpower options only for 5.0.1+.\n    \n  Let me structure:\n  - characteristics.type_level: battery_voltage: {3.6, \"V\"}? and battery_type: \"D-cell Lithium\".\n  - config_level: attribute \"power_supply_options\", axis \"software_version\":\n    - condition \"4.3.1_ or lower (and base)\": \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\"\n    - condition \"5.0.1_ or higher\": \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium non-replaceable battery, or 9…30 VDC, or 100…230 VAC / 50-60Hz\"\n  \n  Plus numeric entries:\n  - power_supply_voltage_dc: {min 9, max 30, unit \"V DC\"} (Flexpower option)\n  - power_supply_voltage_ac: {min 100, max 230, unit \"V AC\"}\n  - power_supply_frequency: {min 50, max 60, unit \"Hz\", tolerance ±2%}\n  - battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n  \n  I think capturing these numerically per rule 3 (split voltage/frequency) is good. I'll add:\n  - type_level: battery_voltage: 3.6 V (with footnote about replaceable? no)\n  - type_level: battery_type: \"D-cell Lithium (replaceable); Flexpower non-replaceable option for software 5.0.1_ or higher\" — hmm.\n\n  Let me do:\n  - type_level: \"battery\": {value: \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium\"}? \n\n  Plan:\n  - type_level entries:\n    - battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n    - battery_type: {value: \"D-cell Lithium\"}\n  - config_level entries (axis \"software_version\"):\n    - attribute \"power_supply\": values [{condition: \"all versions\", value: \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\"}, {condition: \"5.0.1_ or higher (Flexpower)\", value: \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium non-replaceable battery, or 9…30 VDC, or 100…230 VAC / 50-60 Hz\"}]\n  \n  Hmm, this duplicates. Alternative simpler: config_level attribute \"power_supply_options\":\n    - condition \"any (replaceable battery)\": \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\"\n    - condition \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher (Flexpower), option 1\": \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium non-replaceable battery\"\n    - condition \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher (Flexpower), option 2\": \"9…30 V DC\"\n    - condition \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher (Flexpower), option 3\": \"100…230 V AC / 50-60 Hz (±2%)\"\n  \n  And numeric structured entries in type_level for the voltage/frequency where unit extraction matters. But type_level implies applies to whole cert — the VAC/VDC options are conditional. Could use config_level for those too:\n\n  config_level:\n  - attribute \"power_supply_voltage\", axis \"supply_type\":\n    - condition \"DC\": {min 9, max 30} V\n    - condition \"AC\": {min 100, max 230} V\n    - condition \"battery\": 3.6 V\n  - attribute \"power_supply_frequency\", axis \"supply_type\": condition \"AC\": {min 50, max 60} Hz\n\n  Hmm, that's a config axis abuse (axis should be configuration dimension; supply_type is a configuration axis of sorts). Or axis \"power_supply_option\". I think that works nicely:\n\n  config_level:\n  - {attribute: \"power_supply_voltage\", axis: \"power_supply_option\", values: [{condition: \"D-cell Lithium battery\", value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"DC supply\", value: {min: 9, max: 30}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}, {condition: \"AC supply\", value: {min: 100, max: 230}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}]}\n  - {attribute: \"power_supply_frequency\", axis: \"power_supply_option\", values: [{condition: \"AC supply\", value: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}]}\n\n  Plus a note that AC/DC options require Flexpower with software version 5.0.1_ or higher. Could add a config_level attribute \"power_supply_alternative_flexpower\" axis software_version... or put in description. I'll add a config_level attribute \"power_supply\", axis \"software_version\", with text values summarizing. Hmm, may be over-engineering. \n\n  Let me simplify: \n  - type_level: battery_voltage {3.6 V}, battery_type \"D-cell Lithium (replaceable)\".\n  - type_level: power_supply_type: \"battery or Flexpower (external power)\".\n  - config_level: axis \"power_supply_option\" for voltage & frequency as above, with conditions noting software version requirement in condition text: \"DC (Flexpower, software 5.0.1_ or higher)\" etc.\n\n  Conditions: \n  - \"Lithium battery (replaceable; all software versions)\"\n  - \"Lithium battery (non-replaceable; Flexpower, software 5.0.1_ or higher)\"\n  - \"External DC (Flexpower, software 5.0.1_ or higher)\"\n  - \"External AC (Flexpower, software 5.0.1_ or higher)\"\n\n  That's faithful. \n\n- Software versions: Table with versions and CRC checksums:\n  - 4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, 4.0.11_, 4.0.12_, 4.2.2_, 4.2.4_, 4.2.5_, 4.2.6_, 4.3.0_, 4.3.1_ → CRC: Not applicable\n  - 5.0.1_ → 4Cb5\n  - 5.0.2_ → 71d5\n  - 5.0.3_ → CFF7\n  - 5.0.5_ → dCAb\n  - 5.1.0_ → Ab62\n\n  This is a matrix — could go in matrix_tables (software identification) and/or config_level attribute \"software_crc_checksum\" axis \"software_version\". I'll add matrix_tables entry \"Software versions and CRC checksums\" with columns software_version, crc_checksum; and rows. The first row has 10 versions sharing \"Not applicable\" — I'll list each version as its own row for clarity? Or keep grouped as string \"4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, ...\". I'll list individually with crc null/\"Not applicable\" — or group. Keeping grouped as one row preserves fidelity; but individual rows are more machine-readable. I'll list individually: rows: [{software_version: \"4.0.4_\", crc_checksum: \"Not applicable\"}, ...]. That's 10 + 5 = 15 rows. Fine.\n\n  Also config_level: attribute \"crc_checksum\" axis \"software_version\"? matrix_tables already captures; adding config_level duplicates. The rule 5 says config axes for varying attributes; but matrix_tables is fine for this. I'll do matrix_tables only for software.\n\n  Note about field current: \"In case of software version 4.3.1_ or lower the Field Current can only be set to 16 mA. For software version 5.0.1_ or higher the different Field Currents can be selected and shall be set to 16 mA.\" → config_level: attribute \"field_current\", axis \"software_version\": [{condition: \"4.3.1_ or lower\", value: 16, unit_symbol: \"mA\"}, {condition: \"5.0.1_ or higher\", value: 16, unit_symbol: \"mA\"}]. Hmm both 16 mA but different semantics (\"can only\" vs \"can select, shall be set to 16\"). Values both 16 mA. I'll include with the two conditions and the value 16 mA each. Value shape: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. OK.\n\n**Table 2/3-4 (family flow rates):** Big matrix: Meter size (DN25..DN600), Accuracy class, Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Ratio Q3/Q1. Multiple rows per DN (different class/ratio combos). This is the model matrix.\n\nModels: Each unique (DN, accuracy class, Q3, ratio) combo? Or model_id = DN + accuracy class + ratio? The variants: e.g. DN25 class 2 ratio 400 with two Q1 rows? Wait DN25 has two rows both class 2 ratio 400: row1 Q1 0.025, Q2 0.04, Q3 10, Q4 12.5; row2 Q1 0.04, Q2 0.064, Q3 16, Q4 20. So two variants of DN25 class 2 with different Q3 (10 vs 16, ratio 400 both). These correspond to different nominal flow Q3 ratings (10 m³/h vs 16 m³/h).\n\nSo the true model variants are combos of (DN, class, Q3, ratio). For DN25: {DN25, class 2, Q3=10, ratio 400} and {DN25, class 2, Q3=16, ratio 400}. For DN65: class2 Q3=63 ratio400; class2 Q3=100 ratio400; class1 Q3=100 ratio250. Etc.\n\nTotal variants: let me count rows: DN25: 2, DN40: 2, DN50: 2, DN65: 3, DN80: 4, DN100: 4, DN125: 4, DN150: 4, DN200: 2, DN250: 2, DN300: 2, DN350: 1, DN400: 1, DN450: 1, DN500: 1, DN600: 1. Total = 2+2+2+3+4+4+4+4+2+2+2+1+1+1+1+1 = 36.\n\nModel_id convention: e.g. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\" or \"DN25/R400/Q3=10\". Let me use something readable: \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\". Hmm. The R60 example used \"Emax=25t\". For R49, models like \"DN25 Q3=10 class 2\". I'll define model_id as e.g. \"DN25_cls2_Q3=10\"? Readability: I'll use \"DN25, class 2, Q3=10\" — commas in id are fine.\n\nModel_level characteristics: q1, q2, q3, q4 (m³/h), ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless), accuracy_class (string), meter_size (DN string). Also indicating range and verification scale interval from Table 3 depend on DN group:\n- DN25, DN40, DN50: min indicating value 99.999.999 m³ (=99,999,999 — European thousands separator! \"99.999.999\" is 99 million 999 thousand 999 → 99999999). Verification scale interval max 0.0001 m³.\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: same indicating range, VSI 0.001 m³.\n- DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 99999999 m³, VSI 0.01 m³.\n- DN500, DN600: 99999999, VSI 0.1 m³.\n\nSo model_level entries for indicating_range_minimum and verification_scale_interval_maximum per model. That would add per-model values for 36 models each... The model_level matrix: attribute indicating_range_minimum with 36 values all 99999999 m³; verification_scale_interval_maximum with values varying by model. That's verbose but correct. Alternatively config_level grouped by DN group. The schema's model_level is attribute × model matrix; Table 3 maps DN→VSI, so per-model values work. But writing 36 entries × 3 attributes = lots of JSON. Hmm. Rules say include only if cert covers >1 model variant — it does. But maybe matrix_tables for Table 3 and reference? The schema wants model_level populated from matrix tables (R137 example populates both matrix_tables AND model_level). \n\nPractical approach: matrix_tables for Table 2 (flow characteristics by meter size — but it's really by variant) and Table 3 (indicating device), plus model_family.models with 36 model_ids, plus model_level entries for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 (36 values each). For accuracy class and indicating device attributes, they're derivable; should I also add model_level for them? accuracy_class varies per model (1, 2, or \"1 or 2\"). I could include accuracy_class in model attributes. Let me include accuracy_class as a model attribute within models[] attributes{} — the schema has models[].attributes{}. I can put accuracy_class, meter_size, verification_scale_interval, indicating_range in each model's attributes. That avoids huge model_level arrays and keeps info. \n\nHmm, but R137 example populated model_level with qmax/qt/qmin per model and models[] without attributes. Either approach valid. Given the volume (36 models), I'll do:\n- models[]: each with model_id, label, attributes {meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, indicating_range_minimum, verification_scale_interval_maximum}\n- model_level: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 arrays (the core flow matrix).\n\nActually, if attributes are in models[], then model_level duplicates. To keep the output manageable and complete, I'll populate model_level for q1..q4 and ratio, and put accuracy class + indicating device attrs in models[].attributes. That follows the R137 example pattern (model_level for flow rates) while covering the rest in attributes.\n\nWait — but should Table 3's verification scale interval be in model_level too? I'll put it in models[].attributes since it's per-DN group. Good.\n\nNow decimals: European decimal commas: 0,025→0.025; 12,5→12.5; 0,0625→0.0625; 31,25→31.25; 0,1575; 78,75; etc. Convert all.\n\nLet me enumerate the 36 variants:\n\nDN25:\n1. DN25, class 2, Q1=0.025, Q2=0.04, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, ratio 400\n2. DN25, class 2, Q1=0.04, Q2=0.064, Q3=16, Q4=20, ratio 400\n\nDN40:\n3. DN40, class 2, Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.1, Q3=25, Q4=31.25, ratio 400\n4. DN40, class 2, Q1=0.1, Q2=0.16, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 400\n\nDN50:\n5. DN50, class 2, Q1=0.1, Q2=0.16, Q3=40, Q4=50, ratio 400\n6. DN50, class 2, Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 400\n\nDN65:\n7. DN65, class 2, Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 400\n8. DN65, class 2, Q1=0.25, Q2=0.4, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 400\n9. DN65, class 1, Q1=0.4, Q2=0.64, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 250\n\nDN80:\n10. DN80, class 2, Q1=0.25, Q2=0.4, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 400\n11. DN80, class 2, Q1=0.4, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 400\n12. DN80, class 1, Q1=0.625, Q2=1, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 160\n13. DN80, class 1, Q1=0.64, Q2=1.024, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 250\n\nDN100:\n14. DN100, class 2, Q1=0.4, Q2=0.64, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 400\n15. DN100, class 2, Q1=0.625, Q2=1, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 400\n16. DN100, class 1, Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 160\n17. DN100, class 1, Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 250\n\nDN125:\n18. DN125, class 2, Q1=0.625, Q2=1, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 400\n19. DN125, class 2, Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 400\n20. DN125, class 1, Q1=1.5625, Q2=2.5, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 160\n21. DN125, class 1, Q1=1.6, Q2=2.56, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 250\n\nDN150:\n22. DN150, class 2, Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 400\n23. DN150, class 2, Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 400\n24. DN150, class 1, Q1=2.5, Q2=4, Q3=400, Q4=500, ratio 160\n25. DN150, class 1, Q1=2.52, Q2=4.032, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 250\n\nDN200:\n26. DN200, class 2, Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 400\n27. DN200, class 1, Q1=3.9375, Q2=6.3, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 160\n\nDN250:\n28. DN250, class 2, Q1=2.5, Q2=4, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 400\n29. DN250, class 1, Q1=6.25, Q2=10, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 160\n\nDN300:\n30. DN300, class 2, Q1=4, Q2=6.4, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 400\n31. DN300, class 1, Q1=10, Q2=16, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 160\n\nDN350:\n32. DN350, class \"1 or 2\", Q1=15.625, Q2=25, Q3=2500, Q4=3125, ratio 160\n\nDN400:\n33. DN400, class \"1 or 2\", Q1=25, Q2=40, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 160\n\nDN450:\n34. DN450, class \"1 or 2\", Q1=25, Q2=40, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 160\n\nDN500:\n35. DN500, class \"1 or 2\", Q1=39.375, Q2=63, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 160\n\nDN600:\n36. DN600, class \"1 or 2\", Q1=63, Q2=100.8, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 160\n\nModel IDs: I'll use \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\" style? Or \"DN25 Q3=10 (class 2)\". Let me pick: model_id: \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\". Hmm, for \"1 or 2\" classes: \"DN350-class1or2-Q3=2500\". A bit awkward. Alternative: \"DN25 R400 Q3 10\"? The ratio is redundant (derivable). I'll use format: \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\", \"DN350-class1or2-Q3=2500\". Labels: \"DN25, class 2, Q3 = 10 m³/h\".\n\nActually maybe simpler ids: \"DN25-2-10\" too cryptic. Go with the descriptive one.\n\nmatrix_tables: \"Flow rates by meter size\" with columns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1; column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]; rows: 36 rows (objects). Good.\n\nTable 3 matrix: \"Indicating device characteristics by meter size\" columns: meter_size_group... rows with meter_sizes list, indicating_range_minimum_value 99999999, verification_scale_interval_maximum 0.0001 etc. Note \"99.999.999\" — thousands separators with dots; parse to 99999999. Unit m³.\n\nAlso software table matrix (as discussed).\n\n**Components?** No sub-assemblies named (Modbus PCB, MCU mentioned only in revision history as alternatives added). Not structured as components. Skip components.\n\n**model_family.family_name:** \"WATERFLUX 3070\" is the type name; family_name: \"WATERFLUX 3070\"? The cert says \"general characteristics of the family of instruments\". Family name could be \"WATERFLUX 3070\". I'll set family_name: \"WATERFLUX 3070\".\n\n**certified_type.module_designation:** Not applicable → null or omit. Omit.\n\n**description:** \"Electromagnetic water meter for cold and hot potable water\" — hmm, cert says type is electromagnetic water meter; recommendation title mentions cold potable water and hot water. Water temperature class T50 (hot up to 50°C). Description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\". I'll include a brief description from doc content: \"An electromagnetic water meter, type WATERFLUX 3070\". Keep it to what's stated: \"An electromagnetic water meter\".\n\n**certificate.number:** \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\" with revision 3 — the number field: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\". Revision? certificate schema has no revision field, but revision_history covers it. Could set number to \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\" and rely on revision_history. Some may want number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02 revision 3\". I'll keep number clean: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\".\n\ncertificate.scheme: \"A\". project_number: \"3846781\" (string or number? probably string). page_total: 6. member_state: \"The Netherlands\". date_issued: \"2024-07-30\". oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\".\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi Certin B.V.\", address_lines [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"], person_responsible \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\", phone \"+31 88 636 2332\", email \"certin@nmi.nl\", website \"www.nmi.nl\", oiml_issuer_id \"NL1\". person_title: not given (\"Person responsible\" has no title; maybe \"Certification Board\"? Not a title). Omit person_title.\n\n**recommendation:** id \"R49-1\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [\"1\",\"2\"]. Amendment: none — omit.\n\n**Footnotes:** none in doc. Omit footnotes or empty array. Omit.\n\n**Revision history changes for Initial: \"-\"** → changes: null. Revision \"Initial\". date 2021-01-26.\n\nDates format: keep as ISO or original? Schema doesn't specify. I'll use ISO dates: 2024-07-30, 2021-01-26, 2022-05-03, 2023-06-12, 2024-07-30. Test report dates: 2009-09-16, 2010-04-09, 2011-05-02, 2012-03-28, 2013-07-18, 2015-03-18, 2016-03-30, 2020-12-30.\n\n**Verification scale interval values:** 0,0001 → 0.0001; 0,001; 0,01; 0,1 m³.\n\n**Table 1 additional:**\n- reverse_flow: I'll write attribute \"reverse_flow\" value \"The sensor is designed to measure reverse flow\" — or boolean-ish \"designed to measure reverse flow\". Keep original-ish trimmed.\n\nNow, type_level characteristics summary:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — hmm, models vary; but overall cert covers 1 and 2. Fine as type_level too. Actually maybe skip in type_level since per-model exists; but keep for completeness. I'll keep.\n- mechanical_environment: \"M2\" (with caveat). Include.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa (and 16 bar alt — I'll add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_alternative\"? Skip... actually let me include second entry \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\"? Ugly. Alternative: value as {min:1.6,max:1.6}? No. Just 1.6 MPa. Hmm, losing \"16 bar\" — it's a conversion, fine to lose.) Actually, I could include it as part of matrix? No. Skip.\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream)\"\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is designed to measure reverse flow\"\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp63\"\n- maximum_pressure_loss: 0.63 bar\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V\n- battery_type: \"D-cell Lithium\"\n- power_supply_type: \"replaceable D-cell Lithium battery; or Flexpower (non-replaceable battery, DC 9–30 V, or AC 100–230 V / 50–60 Hz) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher\" — hmm, this duplicates config_level. Maybe skip power_supply_type and rely on config_level. I'll include a concise config-based structure:\n\nconfig_level:\n1. environmental_class (axis software_version): O / B as above.\n2. temperature_range_ambient (axis software_version): -25/+55 and -10/+55 °C.\n3. field_current (axis software_version): 16 mA both conditions.\n4. power_supply_voltage (axis power_supply_option): battery 3.6 V; DC {9,30} V; AC {100,230} V.\n5. power_supply_frequency (axis power_supply_option): AC {50,60} Hz.\n6. power_supply_option description? Add attribute \"power_supply\" with text conditions:\n   - condition \"Replaceable battery (all software versions)\": \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\"\n   - condition \"Flexpower (software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\": \"Non-replaceable 3.6 V D-cell Lithium battery, or 9…30 VDC, or 100…230 VAC / 50-60 Hz (±2%)\"\n   \n   This is informative. Include as config_level attribute \"power_supply\".\n\nHmm — the ±2% tolerance on frequency: include in condition text or footnote? I'll append \"(±2 %)\" in the frequency condition. Actually for frequency value {50,60} Hz condition \"AC supply (±2 % tolerance)\". OK.\n\nAlso battery replaceable vs non-replaceable distinction: replaceable for base; non-replaceable within Flexpower. In power_supply_voltage config: condition \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium replaceable battery\" value 3.6; condition \"3.6 V D-cell Lithium non-replaceable battery (Flexpower, software 5.0.1_ or higher)\" value 3.6. Fine.\n\nValue shapes in config_level: {condition, value, unit_symbol} — matches schema's \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\". Should they include footnote_markers? Only if needed. OK.\n\nNow model_level arrays with 36 values each for q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio. Let me also decide whether to include accuracy_class in model_level — I'll put in models[].attributes instead.\n\nmodels[].attributes for each model:\n- meter_size: \"DN25\"\n- accuracy_class: \"2\" (or \"1 or 2\")\n- indicating_range_minimum: 99999999 m³\n- verification_scale_interval_maximum: 0.0001 m³ (per group)\n\nWriting attributes for each of 36 models is verbose but thorough. Given output length constraints, this is acceptable — it's valuable data.\n\nHmm, wait. Do I want indicating_range in attributes with unit? attributes{} is free-form: {\"indicating_range_minimum_m3\": 99999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum_m3\": 0.0001}? To keep structured values, maybe attributes: {\"meter_size\": \"DN25\", \"accuracy_class\": \"2\", \"indicating_range_minimum\": {\"value\": 99999999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": {\"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"}}. The schema doesn't dictate the inner shape of attributes{}. I'll use the structured form for consistency.\n\nAlternatively skip models[].attributes and add matrix_tables for Table 3, plus model_level for verification_scale_interval and indicating_range... that's 36×2 more arrays. attributes per model is cleaner.\n\nDecision: models[] entries include attributes {meter_size, accuracy_class, indicating_range_minimum, verification_scale_interval_maximum}. model_level covers q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1.\n\nmatrix_tables:\n1. \"Flow rates of the family of instruments\" — 36 rows, columns [meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1].\n2. \"Indicating device characteristics\" — 4 rows.\n3. \"Software versions and CRC checksums\" — 15 rows (or grouped). I'll do individual rows for each version. Actually first cell lists 10 versions with \"Not applicable\". Let me do 10 rows each \"Not applicable\"? That's fine: crc_checksum: \"Not applicable\" (string).\n\nNow footnotes: none. Omit field entirely? Schema lists footnotes[]; omit since none (rule 8: omit if not present).\n\ntest_reports role: The text: \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". These are OIML Type Evaluation Report(s) — page 1 mentions \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\". Role for all: \"type_evaluation_report\". The documentation folder T10201-11 → role \"documentation_file\", id \"T10201-11\", no date, no pages.\n\nWait, should documentation folder be in test_reports? Schema's test_reports roles include documentation_file, so yes.\n\n**Components:** none. Omit.\n\n**model_level arrays** — let me now write them carefully.\n\nattribute \"minimum_flow_rate\" (q1)? Use canonical: q1, q2, q3, q4? The labels: \"Minimum Q1\", \"Transitional Q2\", \"Permanent Q3\", \"Overload Q4\". Canonical: minimum_flow_rate_q1? I'd use \"q1_minimum_flow_rate\"? Rule 1: canonical labels. I'll use: \"minimum_flow_rate_q1\", \"transitional_flow_rate_q2\", \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\", \"overload_flow_rate_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\". Good.\n\nUnit: m³/h.\n\nLet me draft model_level entries (36 values each):\n\nq1: DN25-class2-Q3=10: 0.025; DN25-class2-Q3=16: